<description>
Select the 3x3 sprite with the largest foreground-pixel count and return that sprite.

Input structure
The input is a one-dimensional strip of 3x3 tiles, either horizontal or vertical.
Black is the background color. Each tile contains exactly one non-black color.

Rule
Partition the strip into its 3x3 tiles. Count non-black pixels in each tile,
require a unique maximum count, and output that winning tile unchanged.

Key insight
The selected sprite is determined by filled area, not by color or by its position
in the strip.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "monochrome 3x3 sprites arranged as a horizontal or vertical strip on black background",
  "transform": "select the unique sprite with the largest foreground-pixel count and return it unchanged",
  "grid_shape": "3x3 selected sprite tile"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: horizontal (3 x 3N) or vertical (3N x 3) strip of 3x3 tiles on black background
  tile_count: 3-5
  orientation: horizontal or vertical, roughly equal likelihood
  tiles:
    size: 3x3 each
    each_tile: exactly one non-black foreground color, with 1+ foreground pixels
    all_colors_distinct: true
    colors: chosen from non-black ARC palette (1-9)
  losers:
    all_share_identical_shape: true (same fg mask, different colors) [observed]
    fg_pixel_count: 4-6
  winner:
    exactly_one: true, has strictly more fg pixels than every loser
    fg_pixel_count: 5-8, strictly greater than loser count
    position: any index among the tiles
  output: the single winning 3x3 tile, returned unchanged

generation_strategy:
  approach: direct construction (constraints are simple enough)
  steps:
    1. Pick orientation (horizontal/vertical) and tile_count (3-5).
    2. Generate a random 3x3 binary loser mask with fg_count in [4,6].
    3. Generate a random 3x3 binary winner mask with fg_count strictly > loser fg_count and <= 8; ensure winner mask differs from loser mask.
    4. Choose tile_count distinct non-black colors; assign one to winner, rest to losers.
    5. Build each loser tile by coloring the loser mask with its assigned color.
    6. Build the winner tile by coloring the winner mask with its assigned color.
    7. Pick a random position for the winner among the tiles.
    8. Concatenate tiles into the strip (horizontal or vertical).
    9. Output is the winner tile.
```
</generation_spec>
</reference_puzzle>

